---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Oreslang Problem Domains"
  description="The domains Oreslang targets: actor runtimes, FaaS, mobile isolation, high-performance computing, data science, web systems, and data-oriented execution."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <p class="kicker">Problem domains</p>
    <h1>Where Oreslang is meant to matter.</h1>
    <p>
      Oreslang targets workloads where safety, isolation, concurrency, runtime performance,
      and code mobility all matter at once—from dense numerical kernels to multi-tenant FaaS runtimes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="page-body">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="status-note">
        Some of these are current implementation domains and some are explicit competitive ambitions.
        “Replacement” targets describe where the project intends to compete; they are not benchmark claims
        that Oreslang has already surpassed mature ecosystems.
      </p>

      <div class="problem-list">
        <article class="problem">
          <h3>High-performance computing + data-oriented programming</h3>
          <p>
            Dense numerical work should be organized around the hardware that executes it. Oreslang is
            exploring columnar and struct-of-arrays layouts, cache-friendly traversal, allocation-conscious
            kernels, SIMD/vectorization targets, bounded regions, and CPU/GPU placement. Actors own and
            orchestrate state; dense kernels do the compute without turning every element into an object.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="problem">
          <h3>Data science — a Python 3 replacement target</h3>
          <p>
            The ambition is Python-class data-science ergonomics with a stronger static and runtime model:
            typed columnar data, numerical reductions, matrices, pipelines, first-class functions, actors,
            ownership, AOT/JIT execution, and future GPU acceleration in one language. Oreslang should be
            able to serve both exploratory data work and production systems without forcing a rewrite at
            the Python/native boundary.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="problem">
          <h3>Erlang / Elixir successor — and a Phoenix-class web target</h3>
          <p>
            Oreslang takes the actor model seriously while adding static typing, ownership and borrowing,
            option types, isolated/untrusted actors, Graal-backed execution, and data-oriented compute.
            The goal is to preserve the strengths that make Erlang and Elixir compelling for supervision,
            concurrency, messaging, and hot code while pushing toward a faster, more statically analyzable
            systems language and a web stack capable of competing with Phoenix.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="problem">
          <h3>Building FaaS platforms — a workerd alternative target</h3>
          <p>
            Oreslang is also a language/runtime substrate for people building serverless platforms, not only
            code that runs on them. Shared actors, isolated actors, untrusted actors, capability gates,
            non-cooperative scheduling, hot-loaded generations, AOT/JIT/hybrid execution, cancellation,
            quotas, and confined memory are all relevant to building dense multi-tenant FaaS systems.
            That puts OresVM on a path toward the problem space occupied by runtimes such as Cloudflare
            workerd, with actors and language-level isolation as first-class primitives.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="problem">
          <h3>Isolation on mobile phones</h3>
          <p>
            Mobile apps increasingly host extensions, automation, downloaded behavior, and concurrent
            background work. Spawning a full operating-system process for every unit is often impractical.
            Oreslang’s actor model is intended to create stronger in-process isolation boundaries, while
            still allowing selected immutable code to be shared instead of duplicated.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="problem">
          <h3>Isolation in FaaS and multi-tenant runtimes</h3>
          <p>
            Serverless hosts need rapid startup, dense packing, cancellation, quotas, and clear tenant
            boundaries. Oreslang actors are intended to map cleanly onto shared, isolated, and untrusted
            execution pools so the runtime can choose the cost of a boundary without changing the application model.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="problem">
          <h3>True data safety</h3>
          <p>
            Memory safety is necessary but insufficient. Oreslang combines ownership/borrowing checks,
            closed structural values, explicit option/result types, immutable-by-default data, and actor
            mailbox sendability rules so data cannot casually retain unsafe pointers or cross trust boundaries.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="problem">
          <h3>Memory-safe actors</h3>
          <p>
            Actor state should be local, messages should have explicit movement/serialization rules, and
            untrusted actors should not gain ambient access to host capabilities. The compiler and VM both
            participate: compile-time checks narrow what is possible, while the runtime owns scheduling,
            GC, cancellation, and enforcement.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="problem">
          <h3>Hot-reloading trusted code</h3>
          <p>
            A running system should be able to load a new immutable code image once and let many actors
            execute it without cloning imports or machine code into every actor. Actor state remains isolated
            while compatible code can be shared inside the same OresVM process.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="problem">
          <h3>Hot-reloading untrusted code</h3>
          <p>
            Untrusted code requires another gate: sharing must be capability- and whitelist-driven.
            The runtime should be able to admit a verified subset of code into shared executable memory
            while keeping untrusted state, capabilities, and mutable references isolated.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="problem">
          <h3>Non-cooperative scheduling</h3>
          <p>
            FaaS and mobile runtimes cannot assume every third-party workload will yield politely.
            Oreslang is designed to support dedicated or isolated execution pools for CPU-hungry actors,
            with cancellation, quotas, and scheduling boundaries enforced by the runtime instead of by convention.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="problem">
          <h3>Actors beyond one machine</h3>
          <p>
            Actor isolation creates a natural future transport boundary. A typed actor reference should
            be able to preserve the same application-facing model while placement moves between local
            memory, an isolated runtime, IPC, TCP, or an external machine. Remote placement is a future
            runtime direction; the surface syntax and scheduling policy are intentionally not frozen yet.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p><a href="/#problem-spaces">← Back to the Problem Domains section on the home page</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
